      "content": "got it. so basically the white patient is the baseline for what we're taught to look for, and everyone else is just a variation or harder to diagnose. does that mean there's a higher risk of mortality or worse outcomes for people of color because the symptoms don't follow the \"standard\" patterns? like, is it mostly just a matter of visual difficulty, or is there something about the biology or the way these groups present that makes them inherently harder to treat?",
